Subject: [PATCH RFC v4 0/3] flow_dissector: byte-identical fast-paths for PPPoE, single-label MPLS, IP-in-IP family

This series extends the v3-namespace per-shape fast-path machinery
(in net-next as commits 74c6ea03a417 .. 8504d0dca46b for the byte-
identical eth_ip / vlan / qinq trio) with three more byte-identical
shapes. Each new shape is its own DEFINE_STATIC_KEY_FALSE, its own
sysctl entry under /proc/sys/net/flow_dissector/, and is gated
independently so operators only enable what their deployment uses.

All three are byte-identical with the slow path's output for the
eligible shape: no behaviour change. The series intentionally
excludes the descent-style behaviour-change patches (the vxlan_inner
RFC EXPERIMENT in v3 and the GRE-inner / Geneve-inner / GTP-U-inner
companions in the parallel Phase E RFC), which carry their own cover
letter so reviewers can review descent semantics separately from the
byte-identical perf wins.

Why these three:

  PPPoE session - the dominant L2 on millions of ISP last-mile
    DSL / FTTH links. The existing slow path's ETH_P_PPP_SES case
    does __skb_header_pointer + per-field switch on every packet.
    Patch 1 replaces it with a straight-line read + tail-call into
    the eth_ip fast-path for the common PPP_IP / PPP_IPV6 inner
    case. PPP_MPLS_UC / PPP_MPLS_MC and PFC-compressed PPP proto
    fields defer to slow path.

  MPLS single-label - the dominant L2.5 on SP backbones. Slow
    path __skb_flow_dissect_mpls() runs per-label even when the
    consumer just wants the label-stack flow key. Patch 2's fast-
    path handles the single-label (BoS=1 on the first label) case
    in straight-line form. Multi-label stacks defer; a depth-2+
    extension can land later, mirroring the vlan -> qinq staging
    in v3.

  IP-in-IP family - IPv4-in-IPv4, IPv6-in-IPv4 (6in4), and the
    IPv6-outer variants. Common in legacy 6to4 tunnels, simple
    GRE-less tunnels, and some VPN gateways. Patch 3 adds a small
    inner helper that the existing v3 v4 / v6 helpers tail-call to
    when the outer protocol/nexthdr is IPPROTO_IPIP / IPPROTO_IPV6
    and the gate is on. The inner overwrites outer addrs/basic
    (matching slow-path behaviour); ENCAP is stamped on
    key_control->flags after the inner returns.

Each patch is self-contained: own DEFINE, own extern in
flow_dissector.h, own sysctl table entry, own Documentation
paragraph under the existing /proc/sys/net/flow_dissector/
chapter that v3 created.

Cost when off: each new shape adds at most a forward not-taken JMP
on the relevant cold path. The hot eth_ip path (TCP/UDP over
plain IPv4/IPv6, the largest share of real traffic) is unchanged.

Base: stacks on flow-dissector-namespace-v3's HEAD-1 (commit
8504d0dca46b, the qinq patch), i.e. the byte-identical subset of
v3. Doesn't depend on the vxlan_inner RFC EXPERIMENT.

Measurements:
  Macro (real workload): captured across the xdp2 testbed fleet -
  4x HP-class x86_64 7.0.x (Zen 1, 10/25 GbE), l-l2 (Zen 2, 25 GbE),
  2x Raspberry Pi 5 (Cortex-A76, 1 GbE), and a Banana Pi BPI-F3
  (SpacemiT K1, RISC-V, 1 GbE) - via the series3-extensions-soak
  orchestrator, which collects per-cell mpstat softirq/sys on the
  receiver alongside iperf3 throughput. The MPLS and IP-in-IP shapes
  run clean across the fleet (status=ok every cell); as with v3,
  these workloads are wire-saturated, so the per-shape macro delta
  is within run-to-run noise and the saving manifests as receiver
  CPU headroom rather than throughput. Full matrices under
  perf-results/ in the xdp2 repo.

  Microbench (dissector cost in isolation): the per-call cost of the
  fast-path mechanism these byte-identical shapes share is quantified
  by the userspace libflowdis A/B in the v3 cover letter - ~47-55%
  reduction across AMD/Intel x86, Cortex-A53/A72/A76, and RISC-V
  SpacemiT X60 (49.8%). The PPPoE / MPLS / IPIP helpers are the same
  straight-line shape as the eth_ip / vlan fast-paths measured there.
  Per-shape microbench isolation is now demonstrated for the v3 shapes
  on RISC-V (eth_ip -29.8%, vlan -33.1%, qinq -35.4%, byte-identical
  verified - saving grows with header complexity; see the v3 cover
  letter / perf-results/2026-06-25-series3-riscv-microbench/). The
  PPPoE / MPLS / IPIP helpers in this series share that straight-line
  shape; their userland ports + per-shape numbers are the remaining
  TODO, tracked in the same harness.

Open questions for reviewers:

  - Should multi-label MPLS be a separate sysctl (mpls_deep, default
    off) or fold into the same `mpls` knob with an auto-toggle
    matching the qinq <-> vlan pattern? Leaning towards "separate
    knob" because vlan/qinq's auto-toggle was driven by a strict
    extension semantic (qinq requires vlan) that doesn't apply here.

  - For IP-in-IP, only the standalone IPIP / IPV6 protocols are
    handled - explicit user-mode tunnels created via ip link add
    type ipip / type ip6tnl. The fast-path does NOT handle
    GRE-encapsulated IP-in-IP (that's the Phase E gre_inner patch).
    Is there a deployment where the standalone IPIP gate is too
    narrow without also fast-pathing GRE? Real-world feedback
    welcome.

  - PPPoE writes FLOW_DISSECTOR_KEY_PPPOE only when the dissector
    requests it; the existing flow_keys_dissector doesn't include
    that key, so the typical hash path skips the write. A user-
    visible doc clarification of "this fast-path is invisible to
    consumers that don't request KEY_PPPOE" might be warranted in
    the Documentation; happy to add if reviewers want it.

